I mean, let's say you're in a relationship with somebody who has a lot of these characteristics. Do you have to behave in that way in order to accommodate the way that they react? Absolutely, absolutely. So the therapy approach that I use most often is called mentalization-based therapy. And this slowing down is really what we call the mentalizing stance. And the mentalizing stance is the stance that we take in order to help another person. feel understood or validated. If you use DBT language, you would say you make another person feel validated. In MBT language, mentalization-based therapy language, we say we make a person

44
